---
import NetworkSimulation from './NetworkSimulation.astro';
import NetworkComparison from './NetworkComparison.astro';
import ModelArchitecture from './ModelArchitecture.astro';
import DiseaseConnectivity from './DiseaseConnectivity.astro';
---

<div class="spwr-case-study">
  <section class="project-context" aria-labelledby="project-context-title">
    <p class="section-label">The challenge</p>
    <div>
      <h2 id="project-context-title">What causes disrupted SPW-R activity in an amyloid mouse model?</h2>
      <p>
        In TgCRND8 mice, I found that SPW-R activity was reduced and that CA1 populations did not
        reorganize normally after experience. Pyramidal neurons were less strongly recruited during
        SPW-Rs, while the underlying coactivity network was already more tightly connected before
        experience. Together, these results pointed to an abnormally rigid hippocampal network, but
        they did not reveal the circuit change responsible. I therefore used computational modeling
        to test two mechanisms associated with Aβ: excess synaptic glutamate and impaired interneuron
        function.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-context" aria-labelledby="model-title">
    <p class="section-label">The model</p>
    <div>
      <h2 id="model-title">Testing circuit mechanisms with a hippocampal network model</h2>
      <p>
        For this work, I collaborated with Jiannis Taxidis, who developed the original
        cortical–CA3–CA1 model with colleagues and supervised the computational experiments.
        I adapted the model to test whether altered glutamatergic or GABAergic transmission
        could reproduce the hippocampal activity patterns I observed in TgCRND8 mice.
      </p>
      <p>
        The model was well suited to this question because it reproduces sharp-wave ripples
        (SPW-Rs) and associated spiking dynamics using empirically constrained parameters.
        It also generates these dynamics during NREM sleep, allowing direct comparison with
        the physiological state represented in my recordings.
      </p>
      <p>
        I focused the analysis on CA1, where simulated firing and coactivity patterns
        could be compared directly with the experimental data.
      </p>
      <aside class="model-panel" aria-label="Computational experiment overview">
        <div class="model-flow">
          <p class="panel-label">Experiment flow</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <span>01</span>
              <div><strong>Establish the baseline</strong><p>Confirm that the circuit generates SPW-Rs and coordinated CA1 firing.</p></div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>02</span>
              <div><strong>Model experience</strong><p>Strengthen connections within a CA3 ensemble and from that ensemble to CA1.</p></div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>03</span>
              <div><strong>Introduce dysfunction</strong><p>Increase excitatory transmission or reduce inhibitory output in the CA3–CA1 circuit.</p></div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>04</span>
              <div><strong>Compare with recordings</strong><p>Ask which manipulation reproduces the CA1 firing and ensemble changes observed in TgCRND8 mice.</p></div>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ModelArchitecture />



  <section class="project-context" aria-labelledby="reading-title">
    <p class="section-label">Interactive Visualization</p>
    <div>
      <h2 id="reading-title">Exploring the baseline network</h2>
      <p>
        The interactive view shows the baseline model at several levels, from individual neurons to
        population activity. Pyramidal neurons provide the network’s excitatory drive, while
        interneurons regulate and synchronize that activity. You can view individual cells by
        type, examine population spiking and firing rates across CA3 and CA1, or select a single
        neuron to inspect its activity and nearby connections.
      </p>
      <p>
        A separate coactivity view highlights a subset of CA1 neurons sampled for analysis.
        These cells are shown in red, with temporary lines appearing between pairs that
        fire within 50 ms of one another. This provides a simple visual representation of
        how coordinated firing emerges across the sampled population.
      </p>
      <p>
        The CA1 current trace provides an LFP-like view of population activity by summing
        modeled synaptic currents at a CA1 sampling site. It is intended as a model analogue
        of pyramidal-layer activity rather than a simulated membrane voltage or measured LFP.
        Connections shown in the interactive view are illustrative; exact network connectivity
        depends on the simulation run.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <NetworkSimulation />

  <section class="project-context" aria-labelledby="reading-title">
    <p class="section-label">Modeling Experience</p>
    <div>
      <h2 id="reading-title">Incorporating experience into the network</h2>
      <p>
        To model the effects of experience, a subset of CA3 pyramidal neurons is strengthened
        within the recurrent CA3 network and in its projections to CA1. This introduces a
        simple form of experience-dependent plasticity, allowing the model to test how prior
        activity reshapes subsequent sharp-wave ripple dynamics and neuronal coactivity.
      </p>
      <p>
        The interactive view compares the baseline and experience-modified networks side by side.
        After strengthening the CA3 ensemble, more CA1 neurons are recruited during SPW-Rs and firing
        increases within ripple events. The coactivity view also shows a larger number of neurons
        firing together, reflecting stronger coordinated activity across the sampled population.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <NetworkComparison />

  <section class="project-context" aria-labelledby="glutamate-comparison-title">
    <p class="section-label">Hypothesis 1</p>
    <div>
      <h2 id="glutamate-comparison-title">Increased glutamatergic transmission from excess synaptic glutamate</h2>
      <p>
        One candidate mechanism for hippocampal dysfunction in Alzheimer’s disease is excessive synaptic glutamate.
        Amyloid-β can disrupt glutamate homeostasis and reduce clearance from the synaptic space, increasing excitatory
        drive and shifting the balance of hippocampal circuits.
      </p>
      <p>
        To test whether this mechanism could reproduce the activity patterns observed in TgCRND8 mice, I increased
        excitatory synaptic strength across the CA3–CA1 network. The model was then run under baseline and
        experience-strengthened conditions using the same plasticity manipulation described above.
      </p>
      <p>
        With elevated excitation, neurons were already more strongly coactive at baseline, and the usual increase in
        coactivity after experience was largely absent. Instead of reorganizing after experience, the network remained
        dominated by strongly coupled activity patterns, resembling the reduced experience-dependent refinement observed
        experimentally.
      </p>
      <DiseaseConnectivity mode="excitation" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <NetworkComparison
    baselineId="def_ca3a31ca1_min_125_max_135_sv0"
    experienceId="exp25%_150_ca3a31ca1_min_125_max_135_sv0"
    catalogPath="data/pfc-hpc/disease-runs.json"
    comparisonId="glutamate-comparison"
    label="Increased glutamatergic transmission: baseline versus experience"
  />

  <section class="project-context" aria-labelledby="inhibition-comparison-title">
    <p class="section-label">Hypothesis 2</p>
    <div>
      <h2 id="inhibition-comparison-title">Impaired interneuron function does not reproduce the coactivity deficit</h2>
      <p>
        A second candidate mechanism was reduced inhibitory control within the hippocampal network. Because interneurons
        regulate pyramidal-cell firing and help organize SPW-R activity, impaired inhibition could in principle alter how
        neuronal ensembles are recruited during ripples.
      </p>
      <p>
        To test this, I reduced inhibitory synaptic strength in the model and again compared baseline with the
        experience-strengthened condition. Although this manipulation changed network activity, it did not reproduce
        the loss of experience-dependent coactivity observed in TgCRND8 mice.
      </p>
      <p>
        Coactivity still increased after experience, indicating that reduced inhibition alone was not sufficient to
        account for the experimental phenotype. In contrast to the elevated-glutamate manipulation,
        the network retained its ability to reorganize following experience.
      </p>
      <DiseaseConnectivity mode="inhibition" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <NetworkComparison
    baselineId="def_ca3a31_20_perc_10_sv0"
    experienceId="exp25%_150_ca3a31_20_perc_10_sv0"
    catalogPath="data/pfc-hpc/disease-runs.json"
    comparisonId="inhibition-comparison"
    label="Reduced inhibitory output: baseline versus experience"
  />

  <section class="project-context" aria-labelledby="next-title">
    <p class="section-label">Conclusion</p>
    <div>
      <h2 id="next-title">Excess excitatory drive reproduces the impaired network response to experience</h2>
      <p>
        The modeling results support excess excitatory drive as a plausible mechanism for the loss of experience-dependent
        network refinement observed in TgCRND8 mice. Increasing glutamatergic transmission produced elevated baseline coactivity
        and reduced the additional reorganization normally induced by experience, whereas weakening inhibition did not reproduce this pattern.
      </p>
      <p>
        This interpretation is also supported by histological evidence showing reduced surface expression of the astrocytic glutamate
        reuptake transporter, consistent with impaired glutamate clearance. Together, these findings point toward disrupted glutamate homeostasis
        as a candidate mechanism linking amyloid pathology to altered hippocampal network dynamics.
      </p>
      <p>
        The simulations do not establish a unique biological mechanism, but they provide a circuit-level hypothesis that can now be tested experimentally.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-context project-context--last" aria-labelledby="next-steps-title">
    <p class="section-label">Next steps</p>
    <div>
      <h2 id="next-steps-title">Toward experience-driven memory formation</h2>
      <p>
        A major next step is to test the model predictions experimentally, particularly whether restoring glutamate clearance or reducing
        excess excitatory drive can recover normal experience-dependent changes in hippocampal coactivity and SPW-R activity.
      </p>
      <p>
        In parallel, I am extending the model toward experience-driven learning rather than imposing synaptic strengthening directly.
        A virtual animal will explore a spatial environment using RatInABox while synaptic connections evolve through spike-timing-dependent
        plasticity (STDP). This will make it possible to examine whether repeated exploration produces stable patterns of coactivity that
        emerge from the animal’s experience.
      </p>
      <p>
        The longer-term goal is to connect behavior, synaptic plasticity, ensemble formation, and ripple-associated reactivation
        within the same simulation, providing a more direct model of how spatial memories are formed and later expressed during offline activity.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>

<style>
  .spwr-case-study { padding-bottom: 12px; }
  .project-context { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 112px minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 28px; padding: 28px 0 var(--project-section-gap); border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .project-context--last { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .section-label { margin: 3px 0 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--project-label-size); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; }
  .project-context h2 { margin: 0; color: var(--text-strong); font-size: var(--project-heading-size); line-height: 1.3; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: -.01em; }
  .project-context div > p { max-width: 620px; margin: 14px 0 0; color: var(--text); font-size: var(--project-body-size); line-height: var(--project-body-leading); }
  .model-panel { margin-top: 24px; border: 1px solid var(--rule); background: #111310; }
  .model-flow { padding: 18px; }
  .panel-label { margin: 0; color: var(--faint); font-size: 9px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .07em; }
  .model-flow ol { margin: 14px 0 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .model-flow li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 28px minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 11px; padding: 11px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--subtle-rule); }
  .model-flow li > span { padding-top: 2px; color: var(--faint); font-size: 9px; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .model-flow strong { display: block; color: var(--text-strong); font-size: var(--project-body-size); font-weight: 500; }
  .model-flow li p { margin: 4px 0 0; color: var(--text); font-size: var(--project-support-size); line-height: 1.6; }

  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .project-context { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 12px; }
    .project-context h2 { font-size: var(--project-heading-size); }
  }
</style>
